// Heritage groups (N1): who people's families are, for partnering and
// names. Data: research/2026-10-01-heritage-and-names.md.
//
// Groups follow the race and Hispanic-origin columns of the Census 2020 name
// files, less "two or more races", which describes people whose parents
// differ. A person's group is their mother's; founders and immigrants have
// their block's. A world may define any groups; their order is their index.
//
// Per group:
// - `founder_share`: relative share of the founders (the population alive in
//   the first year), by that year;
// - `immigrant_share`: relative share of each year's immigrants;
// - `open_market`: per sex, the share of a person's partner seeking that goes
//   to the open market, where partners meet regardless of heritage (the rest
//   stays within the group). A minority group's outmarriage is about this
//   share, since it is a small part of the open market; the majority's follows
//   from the minorities' (each mixed couple has two sides).
// Shares are normalized across groups each year.
//
// Founders, in present-day borders: Gibson & Jung 2002 (Census WP56) for
// White and Black; Nostrand 1975 for about 80k Mexicans in the later
// Southwest; about 450k American Indians within today's borders (1890 Report
// on Indians, Thornton); WP56 with Alaska and Hawaii and Gratton & Gutmann
// 2000 for 1900 and 1950.
//
// Immigrants: DHS Yearbook Table 2, region of last residence by decade,
// mapped to groups, at decade midpoints, corrected for what lawful
// permanent residence records misplace: Mexican arrivals before 1910 (barely
// recorded), Puerto Rican migration to the mainland (US citizens, not in
// DHS), and unauthorized arrivals with the 1989–92 IRCA legalizations moved
// back to the 1970s and 1980s when those people arrived. Check: the
// foreign-born stock by race and Hispanic origin (WP81, 1970–2000).
//
// Fertility and mortality, relative to the base schedules (fertility.ron,
// mortality.ron), which are calibrated to the whole population: a group's
// factor is its rate over the population's average that year (computed from
// the group rates and the composition; research note §5).
// - fertility: total fertility rate. White and Black 1850–1960: Coale &
//   Zelnik 1963, Coale & Rives 1973 (via Haines, EH.Net); 1970–80: NCHS;
//   1980–2024 by race and Hispanic origin: NVSR "Births: Final Data". White
//   before 1980 includes Hispanics. Hispanic before 1980 assumed at the 1980
//   Mexican-origin level; Asian 1.0 and AIAN 1.2 before 1980 (no series).
//   Applies to births in unions (by tilting the parity distribution, which
//   also lowers childlessness, as in CPS 2024: Hispanic 9% against White 18%)
//   and outside them alike.
// - mortality: `adult` scales the background and old-age terms, `infant`
//   the infant term. Black/White age-adjusted death-rate ratios 1900–2010
//   (NCHS, nonwhite before 1970) and infant mortality ratios (Historical
//   Statistics B 142–144); 2023 ratios to NH White by group (NCHS Data Brief
//   521: Hispanic 0.73, Black 1.20, Asian 0.51, AIAN 1.40). Death-rate ratios
//   weight old ages and understate the young-age excess behind e0 gaps, so
//   the adult factors of Black, AIAN, Hispanic (2006+) and Asian (2019+) are
//   solved to reproduce each year's e0 gap to White
//   (examples/calibrate_group_mortality.rs). Before 1900:
//   enslaved e0 about 23 against White 39.5 (Haines, Steckel). AIAN
//   1840–1880: crisis mortality for the population's fall from about 450k to
//   248k (1890 Report on Indians); provisional, calibrated on the 1900 share.
//   Hispanic and Asian before 1970 assumed near White.
//
// Open market (newlywed intermarriage, Pew 2010/2012/2017; Fryer 2007;
// Census couples): Black about 1% before 1960, then women 3 / men 8% (1980),
// 9 / 22 (2008), 12 / 24 (2015); Asian 10–15% in 1940–70 with men above
// women until 1960, then women 39 / men 26 (1980), 40 / 20 (2008), 36 / 21
// (2015); Hispanic 26% (1980), 29% (2008), 27% (2015), about symmetric;
// AIAN 52–58% recently; White newlyweds 1.3% (1960), 4% (1980), 11% (2015).
// Calibrated in examples/heritage_report.rs: the shares above are the
// result of matching the model's new unions (marriage and cohabitation) to
// these newlywed rates; arriving couples (same group) dilute minority rates,
// so Asian and Hispanic shares sit above their targets.
(
    groups: [
        (
            id: "white",
            name: "White",
            description: "non-Hispanic White",
            founder_share: [(1840, 80.65), (1900, 87.08), (1950, 87.17)],
            immigrant_share: [
                (1845, 98.4), (1855, 97.6), (1865, 96.4), (1875, 94.1), (1885, 97.9),
                (1895, 97.8), (1905, 94.2), (1915, 90.6), (1925, 82.1), (1935, 88.0),
                (1945, 68.0), (1955, 63.0), (1965, 48.6), (1975, 22.7), (1985, 16.0),
                (1995, 19.0), (2005, 19.0), (2015, 21.0), (2022, 18.0),
            ],
            open_market: (
                female: [(1840, 0.01), (1960, 0.03), (1980, 0.10), (2008, 0.20), (2015, 0.25), (2100, 0.30)],
                male: [(1840, 0.01), (1960, 0.03), (1980, 0.10), (2008, 0.20), (2015, 0.25), (2100, 0.30)],
            ),
            fertility: [(1850, 0.934), (1870, 0.920), (1880, 0.915), (1900, 0.937), (1910, 0.964), (1920, 0.986), (1930, 0.980), (1940, 0.972), (1950, 0.969), (1960, 0.971), (1970, 0.969), (1980, 0.918), (1990, 0.889), (2000, 0.908), (2007, 0.901), (2010, 0.927), (2015, 0.951), (2019, 0.942), (2024, 0.956)],
            mortality: (
